! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1260 TEST 7 -- "CHK UPPER BOUNDARY DATA",
! the per-bit data test of the UPPER BOUNDARY register.  PDF p.65
! (printed p.62), octal 022215.00-022224.40, transcribed VERBATIM at
! the listing's own addresses, checked bit-exactly by
! verify_ax5b_16099_listing.py AND RUN ON THE MODEL.
!
! THIS IS THE DRIVER docs/NEXT.md ASKED FOR, and finding it corrects
! the guess the file warned about.  Item 1 said: "pp.66-79 were
! GUESSED to hold the positive boundary-interrupt cases.  p.66 read
! first-hand is the tail of a per-bit error table, 'UPPER BOUNDARY
! DATA FAILURE, BIT 9'.  That guess came from a scouting pass's
! inference, not from a page.  FIND THE DRIVER BEFORE TRANSCRIBING."
!
! The driver is here, at 16099/160100 on p.65, and what it drives is
! NOT an interrupt test.  It is a DATA-PATH test of the boundary
! register.  Mapped 2026-09-23 by scanning pp.46-86 for BOUND/MONITOR
! and then reading the hits:
!
!     p.65   "TEST 7 CHKS BOUNDARY CONTROL." / "CHK UPPER BOUNDARY
!            DATA."  -- this block
!     p.67   "CHK LOWER BOUNDARY DATA." and "CHK BOUNDARY CONTROL BIT."
!     p.71   "FETCH FROM X-1 TO CHK UB COMP." and "FROM X-3 TO CHK LB
!            COMP."  <- the COMPARISON tests, i.e. the positive
!            address-monitoring cases the plan was actually after
!
! So pp.65-70 test whether the REGISTERS HOLD THEIR BITS, and p.71
! tests whether the comparison FIRES.  The plan's "pp.66-79" was wrong
! in range and in kind.
!
! WHAT THE BLOCK DOES.  Eighteen iterations -- the upper boundary is
! word 3 bits 0-17, which is exactly eighteen bits:
!
!     LCI,$X1,18            the loop count
!     160100 Z,$MASK        clear the mask each time round
!     LV,$X2,BIT0(X1)       take the word whose only set bit is bit X1
!     SV,$X2,$UB            write it into the upper boundary
!     NOP NOP NOP NOP       four, to let the write land
!     LV,$X2,$UB            read it back
!     KV,$X2,BIT0(X1)       compare against what went in
!     BXE,$+1.0             equal -> skip the error branch
!     B,160B17(X3)          "-DATA ERROR." -- into the per-bit table
!     160101 V+I,$X3,1.32   step to the next error entry (3 half-words)
!     CB+,$X1,160100        count and loop
!
! and 160B17's entries are "UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 0." up to
! BIT 17, three half-words each -- which is why X3 advances by 1.32.
!
! THE FOUR NOPs ARE THE DECK'S, not padding added here.  They sit
! between the store into word 3 and the read back, and they are the
! same shape as routine 1268's store-close-to-fetch spacing.
!
! AND IT CATCHES A BOUNDARY FAULT AND NAMES THE BIT.  Measured
! 2026-09-23.  StretchStorage.bsv holds word 3 in a dedicated register
! ("addrUpperBoundary: boundaryReg <= v"), so dropping one bit of it
! is exactly the hardware fault this block was written to find.
! Injecting
!     addrUpperBoundary: boundaryReg <= (v & ~(64'b1 << 46));
! -- IBM bit 17, the top of the upper boundary field -- halts the
! program at 022263.00, which is 160B17's EIGHTEENTH entry, "UPPER
! BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 17".  Clean, it halts at PASS.  A 1961
! diagnostic identifying which bit of a 2026 model's register failed.
!
! THE TABLE'S SIZE IS LOAD-BEARING, and this was got wrong first.  The
! branch is "B,160B17($X3)" -- INDEXED -- so a failure on bit N lands
! at 160B17 + N*1.32.  A first version of this transcription collapsed
! the eighteen-entry table into ONE hang; the bit-17 injection then
! branched past its end into untouched storage, which decodes as BE,0
! (docs/NEXT.md's trap list) and ran the counter to 0.  The deck steps
! $X3 by 1.32 precisely because each entry is three half-words; keep
! the shape and the halt address names the bit, discard it and the
! test can only say "something broke".
!
! WHY IT RUNS DISABLED.  Word 3 is one of Reference Manual p.31's
! permanently protected locations, so an enabled store into it raises
! DS and is suppressed.  Disabled is the reset state (p.53), and this
! block never enables.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS: three.
!   1. Numeric labels prefixed "L" (16099, 160100, 160101, 160B17).
!   2. 160B17, the per-bit error table, is a "B,$" HANG at its printed
!      address 022231.40 -- so a failure halts there and the PC names
!      it.  The real table's eighteen entries are not transcribed.
!   3. A "BEW,$" exit at 022225.00, where the listing continues with
!      "B,$+1.0".
! BIT0..BIT17 are DERIVED from p.114's table, which prints the rule
! ("BIT<n> is the word whose only set bit is bit n", BIT0 at
! 024522.00, one word per bit) rather than being transcribed row by
! row -- stated so the derivation is visible.

BIT0    SYN, (8)24522.0
L160B17 SYN, (8)22231.40

        SLC, (8)22215.0
L16099  LVI, $X1, 0.0              ! -TEST 7 CHKS BOUNDARY CONTROL.
        LCI, $X1, 18               ! -CHK UPPER BOUNDARY DATA.
        LVI, $X3, 0.0
L160100 Z,   $MASK
        LV,  $X2, BIT0($X1)
        SV,  $X2, $UB              !  write bit X1 into the upper boundary
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        LV,  $X2, $UB              !  read it back
        KV,  $X2, BIT0($X1)
        BXE, $+1.0                 !  equal -> skip
        B,   L160B17($X3)          ! -DATA ERROR.
L160101 V+I, $X3, 1.32
        CB+, $X1, L160100
!
! ---- substitution 3: the exit ---------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)22225.0
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- substitution 2: the error table, EIGHTEEN entries -------------
! The deck's 160B17 has one three-half-word entry per bit, which is
! why the driver steps $X3 by 1.32.  A single hang is NOT enough: the
! branch is INDEXED, so a failure on bit N lands at 160B17 + N*1.32.
! Measured the hard way -- with one hang, an injected bit-17 fault
! branched into untouched storage, which decodes as BE,0 (see
! docs/NEXT.md's trap list) and ran the counter to 0.  With all
! eighteen, the halt address NAMES THE BIT, exactly as the real table
! does.
        SLC, (8)22231.40
E0      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 0
        SLC, (8)22233.0
E1      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 1
        SLC, (8)22234.40
E2      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 2
        SLC, (8)22236.0
E3      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 3
        SLC, (8)22237.40
E4      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 4
        SLC, (8)22241.0
E5      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 5
        SLC, (8)22242.40
E6      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 6
        SLC, (8)22244.0
E7      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 7
        SLC, (8)22245.40
E8      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 8
        SLC, (8)22247.0
E9      B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 9
        SLC, (8)22250.40
E10     B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 10
        SLC, (8)22252.0
E11     B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 11
        SLC, (8)22253.40
E12     B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 12
        SLC, (8)22255.0
E13     B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 13
        SLC, (8)22256.40
E14     B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 14
        SLC, (8)22260.0
E15     B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 15
        SLC, (8)22261.40
E16     B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 16
        SLC, (8)22263.0
E17     B, $                       ! UPPER BOUNDARY DATA FAILURE, BIT 17
!
! ---- BIT0..BIT17, derived from p.114's rule -------------------------
        SLC, (8)24522.0
B0      DD(BU,64), 9223372036854775808  ! BIT0
B1      DD(BU,64), 4611686018427387904  ! BIT1
B2      DD(BU,64), 2305843009213693952  ! BIT2
B3      DD(BU,64), 1152921504606846976  ! BIT3
B4      DD(BU,64), 576460752303423488  ! BIT4
B5      DD(BU,64), 288230376151711744  ! BIT5
B6      DD(BU,64), 144115188075855872  ! BIT6
B7      DD(BU,64), 72057594037927936  ! BIT7
B8      DD(BU,64), 36028797018963968  ! BIT8
B9      DD(BU,64), 18014398509481984  ! BIT9
B10     DD(BU,64), 9007199254740992  ! BIT10
B11     DD(BU,64), 4503599627370496  ! BIT11
B12     DD(BU,64), 2251799813685248  ! BIT12
B13     DD(BU,64), 1125899906842624  ! BIT13
B14     DD(BU,64), 562949953421312  ! BIT14
B15     DD(BU,64), 281474976710656  ! BIT15
B16     DD(BU,64), 140737488355328  ! BIT16
B17     DD(BU,64), 70368744177664  ! BIT17
        END, L16099
